  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{1+3}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+3}-2)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x+3-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\sqrt{x+3}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3}+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
